Thanks, Martin (and Bert, who replied off-list).

If I can still do high school algebra (don't hold your breath...), the
equation to solve is ax^2 + bx + c = 0, where

a = 1/s1^2 - 1/s^2 (Thus, as Martin said, reduces to linear if s1=s2.)
b = -2 * (m1/s1^2 - m2/s2^2)
c = log s1 - log s2 + m1^2/s1^2 - m2^2/s2^2

Cheers,
Andy

