Have you considered the following: 

fit <- lm(T ~ (h1+h2+h3)^3+T*(h1+h2+h3)^3, DF),

where DF = a data.frame with columns T, h1, h2, and h3?  This will 
estimate a0, a1, ..., a7, (-b1), (-b2), ..., (-b7).  With this, I might 
try the following: 

library(MASS)
fit0 <- lm(T~1, DF)
max.model <- T ~ (h1+h2+h3)^3+T*(h1+h2+h3)^3
fit <- stepAIC(fit0, max.model)

     If this is a heat transfer problem, do you have a corresponding 
system of differential equations?  If yes, is it linear?  If yes, have 
you considered the package "odesolve" that is (or was) downloadable from 
"www.r-project.org"?  If the system is nonlinear, I might search the 
Scilab archives "www.scilab.org" to see what I could find there. 

     At minimum, "stepAIC" should get you beyond where you are now.  
With luck, considering differential equations might even bring you 
closer to the problem that really interests you. 

     Comments?
     spencer graves

